WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:02.790 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  growing defiance  a consecutive sunday of historic  protests calling for their leader to go  standing firm despite threats of a new  crackdown  they want fair elections not what they  see as the fast that happened two weeks  ago  [Applause]  i think that everyone here on the square  is afraid and there is a reason to be  scared  because our government has already shown  how it can treat its people  but there is faith more faith and fear  so that's why we came here today  but president alexander lukashenko is  standing firm  too in the face of this challenge his  military  for the first time saying they'll guard  certain sites from protesters not the  police  a potentially sinister sign the way i  read the  most recent speech of the defense  minister is  as a uh a preparation  of his subordinates uh to be used  against uh what they would see as  domestic enemies which of course are our  own peaceful citizens  so martial law or something like it  would be a next major escalation on the  part of the authorities  [Applause]  and yet the sea of white and red flows  on  [Applause]  the baltic states linked arms 31 years  ago  in peaceful protest against soviet rule  it was a historic  milestone on their road to independence  and they believe history is being made  again  this time in belarus that's why tens of  thousands of people here in lithuania  have come together to reenact that human  chain  stretching from here close to the border  all the way to the capital vilnius  under pressure belarus's president has  even accused lithuania  and other nato allies of posing a threat  this is not not tanks nothing to do with  militaries  peaceful people with blacks flowers same  people  away on the streets of minsk carrying  flowers so  this rhetoric i would explain just for  domestic use  [Applause]  but back in belarus will people power  alone be enough to topple the regime  or will the president again use force  to beat back this call for change debra  haynes sky news vilnius

